From ubf72801fc1b5c1cc at groups.google.com  Tue Jan 14 06:28:26 2014
From: ubf72801fc1b5c1cc at groups.google.com (Daniel Fulop)
Date: Tue, 14 Jan 2014 06:28:26 +0000
Subject: Mapping in a BC2, BC3 mixed population.
Message-ID: <NSsWyJWwAl0.EqQeVR46wgMJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl-disc/c/NSsWyJWwAl0/m/EqQeVR46wgMJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,

I am analyzing data from a large plant population of 476 inbred lines.  The population was made by our colleagues and comprises a mix of BC2 and BC3 lines that were inbred for 4 generations before we sequenced and phenotyped them.

We know which lines are BC2 and which BC3.  I know such a mixed crossing scheme presents complications for QTL mapping, but nevertheless was hoping to analyze our data with R/qtl.

As a first stab I am thinking of analyzing the data twice, once specifying the crossing scheme as BC2 and another as BC3.  However, since we know which lines are BC2 and BC3, would it be possible to analyze the data as a BC2/BC3 combination?

I realize the answer is probably no, at least not without extending the capabilities of R/qtl.  Nonetheless, I wonder if it's at all feasible to do so, and how hard such an extension of the codebase might be.

Any other suggestions or advice regarding mapping with this mixed scheme would be greatly appreciated!!

Thanks in advance for your help!!
Dan Fulop.

From u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com  Tue Jan 14 06:59:38 2014
From: u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com (Karl Broman)
Date: Tue, 14 Jan 2014 06:59:38 +0000
Subject: Re: [Rqtl-disc] Mapping in a BC2, BC3 mixed population.
Message-ID: <NSsWyJWwAl0.UE_bG5R5CDkJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <NSsWyJWwAl0.EqQeVR46wgMJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
References: <NSsWyJWwAl0.EqQeVR46wgMJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl-disc/c/NSsWyJWwAl0/m/UE_bG5R5CDkJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The main concern I would have would be handling possible relationship structure (that they’re probably not all equally related).

